    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "paragraph_number": 132,
      "text": "Higher AUROC and AP and lower Brier scores indicate better predictive performance. Ideal values are 0 for CITL and 1 for the calibration slope. Values are point estimates with 95% CIs. Internal validation includes 133 patients; external evaluation includes 102 hospitalization episodes from 50 patients. External confidence intervals accounted for repeated episodes within patients. AUROC, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve; AP, average precision; CITL, calibration-in-the-large. CI, confidence interval; LASSO, least absolute shrinkage and selection operator; RBF-SVM, radial-basis-function support vector machine; XGBoost, Extreme Gradient Boosting; LightGBM, Light Gradient Boosting Machine.",
      "display_html": "Higher AUROC and AP and lower Brier scores indicate better predictive performance. Ideal values are 0 for CITL and 1 for the calibration slope. Values are point estimates with 95% CIs. Internal validation includes 133 patients; external evaluation includes 102 hospitalization episodes from 50 patients. External confidence intervals accounted for repeated episodes within patients. AUROC, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve; AP, average precision; CITL, calibration-in-the-large. CI, confidence interval; LASSO, least absolute shrinkage and selection operator; RBF-SVM, radial-basis-function support vector machine; XGBoost, Extreme Gradient Boosting; LightGBM, Light Gradient Boosting Machine."
